{"what_the_paper_shows":"The attached PDF contains the main text, Methods, Extended Data Figs 1–4 and the Reporting Summary, but not the Supplementary Information, so Table A1.2 cannot be inspected directly. On validation, the paper says the following. The outcome is binary: regeneration did or did not occur over 2000–2016, from Fagan et al. The random forest uses 2000-era covariates (Methods, 'Modelling approach'; 'Tree cover'). Validation used 'an independent set of random points that was balanced with respect to the dependent variable' (4.87 M points, 87.9% accuracy; p. 133; Extended Data Fig. 4). Only after this were three covariates updated to produce the present/2030 map: forest density and distance to forest from 2018 tree cover, and land cover from 2015 ESA CCI. The paper states that 2030 predictions assume '2000 to 2016 conditions apply to future scenarios'. The autocorrelation check (Extended Data Fig. 4c) shows accuracy falling to about 75% at 6–8 km from training points under balanced bootstrap sampling. Accuracy is reported only on 50/50 balanced samples.","is_the_critique_right":"partly right","reasoning":"The substance is correct. The only validation reported is against the same 2000–2016 regrowth period used to train the model. There is no temporal holdout, for example training on 2000–2008 and testing on 2008–2016 or later regrowth, and no field validation. So the 87.9% figure says nothing directly about predictive skill for the updated present/2030 map, which is the product the paper markets.

The specific mechanics in the critique cannot be verified from the attached document, and the Methods suggest they may be misstated. The text implies validation was done with the 2000-covariate model before covariates were updated, not by scoring the 2030 map against 2000–2016 regrowth. That literal comparison would also be partly incoherent, because regrown pixels are masked as forest by 2018.

Related weaknesses visible in the paper sharpen the point. Validation points are randomly placed with no spatial blocking, and regrowth patches average 1.2 ha, so many validation points sit close to training points. Extended Data Fig. 4c shows accuracy degrading to about 75% at greater distances. Accuracy on balanced samples does not test whether the probabilities are calibrated. That matters because the 215 Mha headline is a sum of per-pixel probabilities on a domain where true prevalence of regrowth is very low.","how_much_it_matters":"important","how_much_it_matters_explanation":"The paper uses the 87.9% accuracy to claim its 30-m maps are reliable for planning (p. 136), and it frames outputs for 2015/2030. Without temporal or spatially blocked validation, and without a calibration check, readers cannot judge forward skill or whether the summed-probability area and carbon totals (215 Mha, 23.4 Gt C) are biased. This weakens confidence in the headline figures and the 'high accuracy' language. It does not by itself invalidate the qualitative findings, such as the dominance of proximity to forest and the countries with most potential.","would_you_have_raised_it":"Yes, as a top-tier concern. I would frame it more broadly than the evaluator: the absence of temporal and spatially blocked validation, plus no probability calibration check, even though summed probabilities drive the area and carbon estimates. I would rank it alongside the non-stationarity assumption (2000–2016 conditions carried to 2030) and the low area-based producer's accuracy of the regrowth training map (18.7% in the humid biome)."}
